13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1b",null,{"guide":{"date":"September 13–14, 1862","place":"Western Maryland from Frederick and Middletown across South Mountain toward Boonsboro, with the episode’s combat centered on Fox’s Gap along the Old Sharpsburg Road","result":"Morning United States success at Fox’s Gap. By shortly after noon on September 14, Jacob D. Cox’s Kanawha Division had broken Samuel Garland’s brigade and occupied the pass. Cox then halted to consolidate and await reinforcements. The episode ends during that lull, before the later fighting at Fox’s, Turner’s, Frosttown, and Crampton’s gaps decided the Battle of South Mountain","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"McClellan’s September 13 plan, Lee’s emergency response, and the morning fight at Fox’s Gap on September 14","note":"The episode ends early in the afternoon as both armies reinforce the mountain. The rest of the battle is reserved for the next episode."},{"label":"Battle date","value":"Sunday, September 14, 1862","note":"South Mountain was the first major battle of the Maryland Campaign and was fought three days before Antietam."},{"label":"Operational crisis","value":"The Army of Northern Virginia remained divided while the Army of the Potomac advanced west from Frederick","note":"Lee’s detachments around Harpers Ferry had not completed their assignment. McClellan’s possession of Special Orders No. 191 revealed the separation and created an opportunity to defeat the Confederate columns before they reunited."},{"label":"South Mountain barrier","value":"A north–south ridge crossed by a limited number of usable gaps","note":"The National Road crossed at Turner’s Gap. The Old Sharpsburg Road crossed at Fox’s Gap less than a mile to the south, and the Burkittsville Road crossed at Crampton’s Gap about six miles farther south."},{"label":"McClellan’s main movement","value":"Burnside’s Right Wing toward Turner’s and Fox’s gaps, supported by Sumner’s Center Wing","note":"Jesse Reno’s IX Corps led the advance, with Joseph Hooker’s I Corps following. McClellan accompanied this part of the army."},{"label":"McClellan’s decisive assignment","value":"William B. Franklin’s VI Corps through Crampton’s Gap","note":"Franklin was to enter Pleasant Valley, threaten the rear of Lafayette McLaws’s force at Maryland Heights, help relieve Harpers Ferry, and either turn north or block a Confederate withdrawal as circumstances required."},{"label":"Federal estimate error","value":"McClellan incorrectly estimated roughly 60,000 Confederates near Boonsboro","note":"He expected Longstreet to be near Boonsboro, although Longstreet had gone to Hagerstown and D. H. Hill’s division formed the rear guard."},{"label":"Stuart’s initial assessment","value":"Crampton’s Gap appeared to be the greater danger","note":"J. E. B. Stuart believed McClellan’s main purpose was to relieve Harpers Ferry. He told D. H. Hill that one brigade should be enough at Turner’s Gap and then rode south."},{"label":"Warning from the campfires","value":"Alfred Colquitt recognized a major Federal concentration","note":"On the night of September 13, Colquitt saw thousands of Federal campfires around Middletown and asked Hill for help at Turner’s Gap."},{"label":"Lee changes course","value":"Longstreet was ordered back from Hagerstown and Hill was ordered to direct the defense","note":"Lee had not intended to make a stand on South Mountain. The rapid Federal advance forced him to hold the gaps long enough to protect the separated portions of his army."},{"label":"Threat to McLaws","value":"A Federal breakthrough at Crampton’s Gap could cut him off","note":"Lafayette McLaws commanded Confederate troops on Maryland Heights as part of the Harpers Ferry operation. Lee repeatedly warned him to watch his rear."},{"label":"Hill’s rear guard","value":"Five veteran brigades, about 7,000–8,000 men in the episode","note":"Hill’s division had been posted near Boonsboro under Special Orders No. 191. Much of it was not yet at the gaps when he reached Turner’s Gap around 5:30 a.m."},{"label":"Initial defense","value":"Two brigades and two batteries for a front nearly five miles wide","note":"Hill initially had the brigades of Alfred Colquitt and Samuel Garland and the batteries of John Lane and Jefferson W. Bondurant while also accounting for Orr’s, Frosttown, Turner’s, and Fox’s gaps."},{"label":"Fox’s Gap outpost","value":"About 250 cavalrymen and two guns before Garland arrived","note":"Thomas L. Rosser’s 5th Virginia Cavalry detachment and two guns commanded by John Pelham held the gap. The Daniel Wise farm stood at the summit amid wooded, broken terrain."},{"label":"Garland’s force","value":"About 1,100 fit men in five North Carolina regiments","note":"The brigade was stretched across difficult ground to cover multiple approaches. Hill ordered Garland to hold Fox’s Gap while Colquitt defended closer to Turner’s Gap."},{"label":"Morning attack begins","value":"About 9:00 a.m. at Fox’s Gap","note":"The 23rd Ohio of Eliakim P. Scammon’s brigade, Cox’s Kanawha Division, encountered the 5th North Carolina on Garland’s right."},{"label":"Rutherford B. Hayes wounded","value":"Shot through the left arm above the elbow","note":"The lieutenant colonel of the 23rd Ohio was hit while ordering a charge. Lt. Benjamin Jackson helped move him to cover. Hayes retained a stiff arm but avoided amputation and became the nineteenth United States president in 1877."},{"label":"Samuel Garland killed","value":"Mortally wounded by a bullet through the chest","note":"Garland was struck while directing his brigade near the Wise farm after Col. Thomas Ruffin of the 13th North Carolina was wounded in the hip. Col. Duncan K. McRae assumed brigade command."},{"label":"Confederate line breaks","value":"About 11:30 a.m.","note":"Federal troops pressed Garland’s depleted regiments from the front and around their flanks. The brigade withdrew to avoid encirclement."},{"label":"Fox’s Gap occupied","value":"Shortly after noon","note":"Cox’s division held the summit after roughly three hours of fighting. The action wrecked Garland’s brigade but also exhausted the Federal attackers."},{"label":"Cox pauses","value":"Consolidate the captured ground and wait for reinforcements","note":"Confederate prisoners reported that more of Hill’s division and Longstreet’s command were near. Cox did not immediately turn north against Turner’s Gap."},{"label":"Confederate reinforcements","value":"Rodes and Ripley reached the mountain during the lull","note":"Hill sent Robert E. Rodes north of the National Road and Roswell S. Ripley toward Fox’s Gap after deciding that the Federal threat required more of his division."},{"label":"Episode-end situation","value":"The United States held Fox’s Gap, but the battle remained unresolved","note":"Federal and Confederate columns were still arriving. Fighting later resumed at Fox’s Gap and expanded around Frosttown, Turner’s, and Crampton’s gaps."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“Lee’s Maverick General: Daniel Harvey Hill” by Hal Bridges","note":"The episode recommends the biography for D. H. Hill’s career and perspective."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Burkittsville, Kanawha Division, Lafayette McLaws, Robert E. Rodes, Orr’s Gap, and Jefferson W. Bondurant","note":"The automated transcript produces “Birketsville,” “Canal Division,” “Lafayette McClellan,” “Robert Rhodesman,” “Oar’s Gap,” and “James W. Bondurant.” It also occasionally renders Turner’s Gap as “Kerner’s Gap.”"}],"maps":[{"title":"McClellan’s plan and Lee’s divided army","caption":"On September 13, McClellan directed Burnside’s Right Wing toward Turner’s and Fox’s gaps while Franklin’s VI Corps moved through Burkittsville toward Crampton’s Gap. Hill’s division guarded the Boonsboro approaches, Longstreet had to return from Hagerstown, and McLaws remained near Maryland Heights. Routes and positions are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"U.S. Army Center of Military History, National Park Service, Robert Knox Sneden map, Official Records, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep184-south-mountain-plan.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map of McClellan’s two-pronged advance toward the South Mountain gaps and the separated Confederate commands west and south of the ridge."},{"title":"Morning fight at Fox’s Gap","caption":"The first panel shows Scammon’s brigade of Cox’s Kanawha Division advancing from the east against Garland’s extended North Carolina line near the Wise farm. The second shows the Federal envelopment that forced the brigade back around 11:30 a.m.; shortly after noon Cox held the gap and paused. Unit footprints and terrain are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"U.S. Army Center of Military History, National Park Service, U.S. Army Corps of Topographical Engineers map, Official Records, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep184-foxs-gap-morning.svg","alt":"Two-panel schematic tactical map of the morning fighting at Fox’s Gap, from the first Federal contact around 9 a.m. to the collapse of Garland’s brigade before noon."},{"title":"Robert Knox Sneden’s South Mountain map","caption":"Sneden’s participant-era map shows the relationship among Boonsboro, Turner’s Gap, Fox’s Gap, Frosttown, the National Road, and the Old Sharpsburg Road. Its annotations cover more of the battle than this episode.","source":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division and Virginia Historical Society; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep184-sneden-south-mountain-map.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden’s hand-drawn map of South Mountain showing roads, terrain, buildings, and troop positions around Fox’s and Turner’s gaps."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:11","audioTime":71,"title":"The battle date and purpose are introduced","detail":"The hosts identify September 14, 1862, as the date of South Mountain and place the battle on the road to Antietam."},{"time":"3:31","audioTime":211,"title":"The Lost Order changes the campaign","detail":"Special Orders No. 191 reaches McClellan on September 13 and reveals that Lee’s army is divided."},{"time":"5:03","audioTime":303,"title":"Both commanders face a deadline","detail":"McClellan must cross South Mountain before Lee can reunite his separated columns."},{"time":"6:31","audioTime":391,"title":"South Mountain becomes the physical barrier","detail":"The north–south ridge west of Frederick channels army movement through a limited number of gaps."},{"time":"7:16","audioTime":436,"title":"Turner’s, Fox’s, and Crampton’s gaps are located","detail":"The episode connects each pass to its road and explains that Crampton’s Gap opens into Pleasant Valley behind Harpers Ferry."},{"time":"8:14","audioTime":494,"title":"McClellan forms his plan","detail":"The Army of the Potomac commander uses the information in the Lost Order to plan attacks for September 14."},{"time":"8:29","audioTime":509,"title":"Burnside’s Right Wing takes the northern route","detail":"Reno’s IX Corps leads toward Turner’s Gap with Hooker’s I Corps following and Sumner’s Center Wing in support."},{"time":"9:39","audioTime":579,"title":"Franklin receives the key southern assignment","detail":"VI Corps is to move through Burkittsville, force Crampton’s Gap, and enter Pleasant Valley."},{"time":"10:31","audioTime":631,"title":"McClellan states his objective","detail":"His instruction to Franklin describes a plan to split the enemy and defeat the separated parts."},{"time":"11:48","audioTime":708,"title":"Lee grows concerned at Hagerstown","detail":"Reports from the mountain show that Federal cavalry and infantry are closing faster than expected."},{"time":"12:24","audioTime":744,"title":"Stuart is driven back to the ridge","detail":"Federal cavalry and the front of IX Corps push Confederate horsemen through Middletown toward South Mountain."},{"time":"12:54","audioTime":774,"title":"The Harpers Ferry delay becomes clear","detail":"Stuart learns that the Confederate army remains divided and reports the threat to Lee and Hill."},{"time":"13:35","audioTime":815,"title":"Only Colquitt is sent to Turner’s Gap","detail":"Stuart’s belief that Crampton’s Gap is the primary Federal objective leaves one brigade initially guarding the National Road."},{"time":"13:59","audioTime":839,"title":"Campfires reveal the scale of the threat","detail":"Colquitt looks east from the mountain, sees the Federal concentration around Middletown, and asks Hill for reinforcements."},{"time":"14:17","audioTime":857,"title":"Garland and two batteries move up","detail":"Hill sends Garland’s North Carolinians and the batteries of Lane and Bondurant toward the threatened gaps."},{"time":"14:48","audioTime":888,"title":"Lee recognizes the danger","detail":"The Federal advance is faster than Lee expected while his forces remain separated around Hagerstown, Boonsboro, and Harpers Ferry."},{"time":"15:44","audioTime":944,"title":"Longstreet is ordered back","detail":"Lee directs Longstreet to march from Hagerstown toward Boonsboro at first light so his troops can reinforce the gaps."},{"time":"16:13","audioTime":973,"title":"Hill must personally defend Turner’s Gap","detail":"Lee orders his rear-guard commander to oversee the mountain defense that had not been part of the original campaign plan."},{"time":"16:53","audioTime":1013,"title":"Hill rides from Boonsboro before sunrise","detail":"The episode follows Hill to the summit as he assumes responsibility for protecting the divided army."},{"time":"17:48","audioTime":1068,"title":"A nearly five-mile front comes into view","detail":"Hill must account for Orr’s, Frosttown, Turner’s, and Fox’s gaps with few troops immediately available."},{"time":"19:13","audioTime":1153,"title":"Garland is sent to Fox’s Gap","detail":"Hill directs the 31-year-old brigadier to hold the southern pass and its road approaches."},{"time":"19:39","audioTime":1179,"title":"Rosser and Pelham hold the outpost","detail":"Garland finds a detachment of the 5th Virginia Cavalry and two guns already at Fox’s Gap."},{"time":"20:50","audioTime":1250,"title":"Gunfire opens around 9 a.m.","detail":"Combat begins on the right of Garland’s extended defensive line near the summit."},{"time":"21:09","audioTime":1269,"title":"The 23rd Ohio meets the 5th North Carolina","detail":"Hayes’s regiment leads Scammon’s brigade and Cox’s Kanawha Division into the fight at Fox’s Gap."},{"time":"22:39","audioTime":1359,"title":"Hayes is wounded during a charge","detail":"A musket ball strikes his left arm above the elbow; he remains exposed until Benjamin Jackson moves him behind a log."},{"time":"25:12","audioTime":1512,"title":"Garland directs the line beside Ruffin","detail":"As the 13th North Carolina exchanges fire with the 30th Ohio, Ruffin is seriously wounded in the hip."},{"time":"25:38","audioTime":1538,"title":"Samuel Garland is mortally wounded","detail":"A bullet passes through the brigade commander’s chest. He is carried from the field and dies soon afterward."},{"time":"26:16","audioTime":1576,"title":"Garland’s brigade gives way","detail":"Federal pressure against the front and flanks breaks the Confederate line around 11:30 a.m."},{"time":"27:40","audioTime":1660,"title":"Cox occupies Fox’s Gap","detail":"Shortly after noon, the Kanawha Division controls the pass after about three hours of fighting."},{"time":"28:16","audioTime":1696,"title":"The Federal advance pauses","detail":"Exhaustion and reports of nearby Confederate reinforcements lead Cox to consolidate rather than immediately turn north."},{"time":"29:02","audioTime":1742,"title":"Rodes and Ripley reach the mountain","detail":"Hill deploys new Confederate brigades north of the National Road and toward Fox’s Gap as the noon lull continues."},{"time":"30:00","audioTime":1800,"title":"Hill explains the purpose of delay","detail":"He intends to hold long enough for Longstreet to arrive and for Jackson to finish the Harpers Ferry operation."},{"time":"31:27","audioTime":1887,"title":"The episode recommends Hal Bridges’s biography","detail":"The hosts recommend “Lee’s Maverick General: Daniel Harvey Hill.”"}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Planned the September 14 advance after receiving Special Orders No. 191 and accompanied the main movement toward Turner’s Gap"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Ambrose E. Burnside","command":"Right Wing","subordinates":"IX Corps under Jesse L. Reno and I Corps under Joseph Hooker; directed toward Turner’s and Fox’s gaps"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Jesse L. Reno","command":"IX Corps","subordinates":"Cox’s Kanawha Division led the corps advance from Middletown; later IX Corps fighting falls outside this episode"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Jacob D. Cox","command":"Kanawha Division, IX Corps","subordinates":"Sent Eliakim P. Scammon’s brigade toward Fox’s Gap, captured the pass by shortly after noon, and halted to consolidate"},{"commander":"Col. Eliakim P. Scammon","command":"1st Brigade, Kanawha Division","subordinates":"Included the 23rd Ohio under Lt. Col. Rutherford B. Hayes and the 30th Ohio engaged against Garland’s brigade"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker","command":"I Corps","subordinates":"Followed IX Corps in Burnside’s Right Wing toward the northern gaps; its principal combat comes after the episode’s noon endpoint"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. William B. Franklin","command":"VI Corps","subordinates":"Assigned to force Crampton’s Gap, enter Pleasant Valley, and threaten the Confederate forces around Harpers Ferry"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner","command":"Center Wing","subordinates":"Directed to support Burnside’s main movement west from Frederick"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Ordered Hill to defend the mountain, warned McLaws, and recalled Longstreet from Hagerstown after recognizing the Federal threat"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Longstreet’s Wing","subordinates":"Turned south from Hagerstown at first light to reinforce Lee’s rear guard near Boonsboro and the gaps"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. D. H. Hill","command":"Hill’s Division and defense of Turner’s and Fox’s gaps","subordinates":"Initially employed Colquitt and Garland, then called forward G. B. Anderson, Rodes, and Ripley as the scale of McClellan’s advance became clear"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Samuel Garland Jr.","command":"Garland’s Brigade, Hill’s Division","subordinates":"Five North Carolina regiments defending Fox’s Gap; Col. Duncan K. McRae assumed command after Garland was mortally wounded"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Alfred H. Colquitt","command":"Colquitt’s Brigade, Hill’s Division","subordinates":"Initially sent alone to Turner’s Gap and warned Hill after seeing the size of the Federal encampment around Middletown"},{"commander":"Brig. Gens. George B. Anderson, Robert E. Rodes, and Roswell S. Ripley","command":"Reinforcing brigades of Hill’s Division","subordinates":"Called from Boonsboro as Hill expanded the defense; Rodes moved north of the National Road and Ripley toward Fox’s Gap"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. J. E. B. Stuart","command":"Cavalry Division and screen east of South Mountain","subordinates":"Initially judged Crampton’s Gap the main danger; left Thomas L. Rosser’s detachment and John Pelham’s two guns at Fox’s Gap"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Lafayette McLaws","command":"Confederate force at Maryland Heights","subordinates":"Continued the Harpers Ferry operation while a breakthrough at Crampton’s Gap threatened his rear"}]}],"people":[{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander who planned to cross South Mountain and strike Lee’s divided army","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander forced to defend the gaps while his army remained separated","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"D. H. Hill","role":"Confederate division commander responsible for delaying the Federal advance at Turner’s and Fox’s gaps","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Jacob D. Cox","role":"United States division commander whose Kanawha Division captured Fox’s Gap before noon","side":"United States"},{"name":"Rutherford B. Hayes","role":"Lieutenant colonel of the 23rd Ohio wounded while leading the opening attack at Fox’s Gap","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep184-rutherford-hayes.jpg"},{"name":"Samuel Garland Jr.","role":"Confederate brigadier whose North Carolina brigade defended Fox’s Gap; killed during the morning fighting","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep184-samuel-garland.jpg"},{"name":"Ambrose E. Burnside","role":"Commander of the Army of the Potomac’s Right Wing advancing toward Turner’s and Fox’s gaps","side":"United States"},{"name":"Jesse L. Reno","role":"IX Corps commander whose lead division opened the fighting at Fox’s Gap","side":"United States"},{"name":"Eliakim P. Scammon","role":"United States brigade commander whose Ohio regiments led Cox’s morning attack","side":"United States"},{"name":"Benjamin Jackson","role":"23rd Ohio lieutenant who moved the wounded Hayes to cover under fire","side":"United States"},{"name":"William B. Franklin","role":"VI Corps commander assigned the key movement through Crampton’s Gap","side":"United States"},{"name":"J. E. B. Stuart","role":"Confederate cavalry commander who screened the ridge but initially misread the main Federal threat","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Alfred H. Colquitt","role":"Confederate brigadier who recognized the strength of the Federal concentration from the campfires near Middletown","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas L. Rosser","role":"Confederate cavalry colonel whose 5th Virginia detachment held Fox’s Gap before Garland arrived","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John Pelham","role":"Confederate artillery officer commanding two guns at the Fox’s Gap outpost","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Duncan K. McRae","role":"Colonel of the 5th North Carolina who assumed brigade command after Garland fell","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Frederick C. Ford","role":"20th North Carolina private whose account describes Garland’s brigade withdrawing under Federal pressure","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Brig. Gen. Samuel Garland Jr.","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commander of Garland’s Brigade, D. H. Hill’s Division","outcome":"Mortally wounded by a bullet through the chest while directing his brigade at Fox’s Gap; carried from the line and died soon afterward on September 14, 1862"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep184-south-mountain-plan.svg","alt":"Schematic regional map of the Federal plan and the divided Confederate commands around South Mountain.","caption":"McClellan sent his main body toward Turner’s and Fox’s gaps and VI Corps toward Crampton’s Gap while Lee recalled Longstreet and tried to protect McLaws and the Harpers Ferry operation.","source":"U.S. Army Center of Military History, National Park Service, Robert Knox Sneden map, Official Records, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep184-foxs-gap-morning.svg","alt":"Two-panel schematic map of Cox’s morning attack against Garland’s brigade at Fox’s Gap.","caption":"Cox’s lead Ohio regiments attacked Garland’s extended line from the east, forced it from the summit by late morning, and occupied Fox’s Gap shortly after noon.","source":"U.S. Army Center of Military History, National Park Service, U.S. Army Corps of Topographical Engineers map, Official Records, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep184-sneden-south-mountain-map.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden’s illustrated map of the roads, terrain, and positions around Fox’s and Turner’s gaps.","caption":"Robert Knox Sneden’s map records the relationship among the mountain gaps, Boonsboro, the National Road, and the Old Sharpsburg Road. It was created during or shortly after the Civil War.","source":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division and Virginia Historical Society; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep184-battle-of-south-mountain.jpg","alt":"Nineteenth-century print showing United States troops attacking Confederate troops at the Battle of South Mountain.","caption":"“The Battle of South Mountain, Md., Sunday, Sept. 14, 1862,” an 1864 print depicting the attack of the 23rd and 12th Ohio against North Carolinians under Garland. The composition is commemorative artwork, not a literal battlefield photograph.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep184-rutherford-hayes.jpg","alt":"Brady-Handy glass-negative portrait of Rutherford B. Hayes in civilian clothing after the Civil War.","caption":"Rutherford B. Hayes led the 23rd Ohio at Fox’s Gap and suffered a serious wound to his left arm. This portrait was made after the war, between 1870 and 1880.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division, Brady-Handy Collection; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep184-samuel-garland.jpg","alt":"Full-length Civil War-era portrait of Confederate brigadier general Samuel Garland Jr. in uniform.","caption":"Samuel Garland Jr. commanded five North Carolina regiments at Fox’s Gap and was killed during the morning fighting.","source":"National Park Service, courtesy of Virginia Military Institute; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 184 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2017/02/26/civil-war-podcast-episode-184/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Harpers Ferry to South Mountain","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/harpers-ferry-to-south-mountain.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: South Mountain battle detail","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=md002"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Maryland and Fredericksburg Campaigns, 1862–1863","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-6.pdf"},{"label":"Official Records, Series I, Volume XIX, Part I: D. H. Hill’s report, pages 1018–1030","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/027/1018"},{"label":"Official Records, Series I, Volume XIX, Part I: Maryland Campaign reports","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/027"},{"label":"Rutherford B. Hayes Presidential Library: Diary and Letters of Rutherford B. Hayes","url":"https://www.rbhayes.org/diary/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Rutherford B. Hayes","url":"https://www.nps.gov/people/rutherford-b-hayes.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Samuel Garland Jr.","url":"https://www.nps.gov/people/samuel-garland-jr.htm"},{"label":"Primary map: Robert Knox Sneden, Battle of South Mountain at Fox’s and Turner’s gaps","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00298/"},{"label":"U.S. Army Corps of Topographical Engineers map of South Mountain positions","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447402/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: 1864 Battle of South Mountain print","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003680934/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Rutherford B. Hayes portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2017893107/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Samuel Garland Jr. portrait record","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/98503412/"},{"label":"Virginia Military Institute Archives: Samuel Garland roster","url":"https://archivesweb.vmi.edu/record.php?ID=271"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Robert Knox Sneden’s South Mountain map",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep184-sneden-south-mountain-map.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Robert Knox Sneden’s South Mountain map at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep184-sneden-south-mountain-map.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden’s hand-drawn map of South Mountain showing roads, terrain, buildings, and troop positions around Fox’s and Turner’s gaps.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Robert Knox Sneden’s South Mountain map"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Sneden’s participant-era map shows the relationship among Boonsboro, Turner’s Gap, Fox’s Gap, Frosttown, the National Road, and the Old Sharpsburg Road. Its annotations cover more of the battle than this episode."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep184-sneden-south-mountain-map.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division and Virginia Historical Society; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States",{"className":"command-column #315f8c","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States"}],[["$","div","Army of the Potomac-Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of the Potomac"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Planned the September 14 advance after receiving Special Orders No. 191 and accompanied the main movement toward Turner’s Gap"}]]}],["$","div","Right Wing-Maj. Gen. Ambrose E. Burnside",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Right Wing"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Ambrose E. Burnside"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"IX Corps under Jesse L. Reno and I Corps under Joseph Hooker; directed toward Turner’s and Fox’s gaps"}]]}],["$","div","IX Corps-Maj. Gen. Jesse L. Reno",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"IX Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Jesse L. Reno"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Cox’s Kanawha Division led the corps advance from Middletown; later IX Corps fighting falls outside this episode"}]]}],["$","div","Kanawha Division, IX Corps-Brig. Gen. Jacob D. Cox",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Kanawha Division, IX Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. Jacob D. Cox"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Sent Eliakim P. Scammon’s brigade toward Fox’s Gap, captured the pass by shortly after noon, and halted to consolidate"}]]}],["$","div","1st Brigade, Kanawha Division-Col. Eliakim P. Scammon",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"1st Brigade, Kanawha Division"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Col. Eliakim P. Scammon"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Included the 23rd Ohio under Lt. Col. Rutherford B. Hayes and the 30th Ohio engaged against Garland’s brigade"}]]}],["$","div","I Corps-Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"I Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Followed IX Corps in Burnside’s Right Wing toward the northern gaps; its principal combat comes after the episode’s noon endpoint"}]]}],["$","div","VI Corps-Maj. Gen. William B. Franklin",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"VI Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. William B. Franklin"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Assigned to force Crampton’s Gap, enter Pleasant Valley, and threaten the Confederate forces around Harpers Ferry"}]]}],["$","div","Center Wing-Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner",{"className":"command-entry","children":["$L1c","$L1d","$L1e"]}]]]}],"$L1f"]}],"$L20"]]]}]
16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"losses","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Important leaders killed or wounded"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"loss-list","children":[["$","article","Brig. Gen. Samuel Garland Jr.",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side confederate","children":"Confederacy"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. Samuel Garland Jr."}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander of Garland’s Brigade, D. H. Hill’s Division","."]}]," ","Mortally wounded by a bullet through the chest while directing his brigade at Fox’s Gap; carried from the line and died soon afterward on September 14, 1862"]}]]}]]}]]}]
17:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"images","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Historical images"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"history-gallery","children":[["$","$L21","/images/ep184-sneden-south-mountain-map.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep184-sneden-south-mountain-map.jpg","alt":"Robert Knox Sneden’s illustrated map of the roads, terrain, and positions around Fox’s and Turner’s gaps.","caption":"Robert Knox Sneden’s map records the relationship among the mountain gaps, Boonsboro, the National Road, and the Old Sharpsburg Road. It was created during or shortly after the Civil War.","name":"Robert Knox Sneden’s illustrated map of the roads, terrain, and positions around Fox’s and Turner’s gaps.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division and Virginia Historical Society; see sources below."}],["$","$L21","/images/ep184-battle-of-south-mountain.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep184-battle-of-south-mountain.jpg","alt":"Nineteenth-century print showing United States troops attacking Confederate troops at the Battle of South Mountain.","caption":"“The Battle of South Mountain, Md., Sunday, Sept. 14, 1862,” an 1864 print depicting the attack of the 23rd and 12th Ohio against North Carolinians under Garland. The composition is commemorative artwork, not a literal battlefield photograph.","name":"Nineteenth-century print showing United States troops attacking Confederate troops at the Battle of South Mountain.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],["$","$L21","/images/ep184-rutherford-hayes.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep184-rutherford-hayes.jpg","alt":"Brady-Handy glass-negative portrait of Rutherford B. Hayes in civilian clothing after the Civil War.","caption":"Rutherford B. Hayes led the 23rd Ohio at Fox’s Gap and suffered a serious wound to his left arm. This portrait was made after the war, between 1870 and 1880.","name":"Brady-Handy glass-negative portrait of Rutherford B. Hayes in civilian clothing after the Civil War.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division, Brady-Handy Collection; see sources below."}],["$","$L21","/images/ep184-samuel-garland.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep184-samuel-garland.jpg","alt":"Full-length Civil War-era portrait of Confederate brigadier general Samuel Garland Jr. in uniform.","caption":"Samuel Garland Jr. commanded five North Carolina regiments at Fox’s Gap and was killed during the morning fighting.","name":"Full-length Civil War-era portrait of Confederate brigadier general Samuel Garland Jr. in uniform.","source":"$undefined","credit":"National Park Service, courtesy of Virginia Military Institute; see sources below."}]]}]]}]
